the shock waves of the covid pandemic  have touched every corner of the globe  but now the latest data are revealing  just how unequal the burden of covid has  been  in this video we're going to walk  through some of those data on two  fundamental issues money and health  this is the pandemic's unequal toll  first  poverty  using world bank data scientists can  model the number of people living below  the poverty line that's on less than two  dollars a day in different parts of the  world  before the pandemic struck it was a  variable picture with regions like  europe and east asia seeing falling  rates of extreme poverty whereas others  like the middle east and north africa  and latin america and the caribbean saw  little change or even increases over  time  but if we look at global numbers we can  see a steady decrease year on year since  2015. that is until the pandemic hit  according to projections based on the  latest data the trend takes a  significant turn in 2020 especially when  compared to forecasts of what might have  happened without the pandemic  in every region we see a striking impact  researchers now estimate that the  pandemic has driven up to 95 million  more people into extreme poverty  the war in ukraine and inflation have  exacerbated this crisis yet further  pushing up prices for food fuel and  nearly everything else  what's more the projections we're seeing  here assume that inflation will impact  all demographics equally  but other more pessimistic projections  paint a different picture  next income  using another world bank dataset  researchers compared average daily  income with a prediction of that income  had the pandemic not happened  across the board from the poorest to the  wealthiest portions of society the  pandemic caused a drop in income  but the picture for these groups changes  a lot when you look at how they've  recovered as time passed  all income brackets have seen some  recovery but the pace of that recovery  varies wildly  the wealthiest have by and large  recouped their losses and are close to  regaining their previous track  but as you move to lower incomes the gap  between where income should be and where  it is widens and widens with the lower  earners having not recouped their losses  at all  next health  aside from the economic shocks of the  pandemic the disease itself had unequal  effects on people's health and data from  the u.s centers for disease control and  prevention lays that bare  the covid death rate for indigenous  communities is more than double what it  is for white people  compared with people of asian descent  the rate is about 180 higher  other ethnic groups also experienced  varying death rates  it's important to note that these data  are age adjusted which means they've  been statistically corrected to allow  for fair comparisons between  demographics with different age ranges  disparities like this have been seen  many times before but the pandemic has  really highlighted them  finally the impact of deprivation on  health  this is another place where there are  many well-known links  social deprivation can be quantified by  looking at a few different things like  access to adequate housing education the  crime rate of an area and so on  these data are from the uk office for  national statistics  across health from liver disease to  cancer to cardiovascular disease even  suicide mortality increases with  deprivation  and covid is no exception  at the peak of the first wave between  march and july 2020 the death rate from  covid in the most deprived areas of  england was double that of the least  deprived regions  the pandemic has exposed huge  inequalities the world over and in some  cases made them worse  as the world slowly starts to recover  from the devastating impacts of covid  it's still not clear how long these  effects will last but so far the data  and scientist models are suggesting that  the pandemic will cast a long shadow
 

